WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:06.650
Eureka moments are few and far between
in the world of science. When they do

2
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:11.420
happen, however, they can turn an
entire field on its head. A compelling

3
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.880
example is the discovery of RNA
interference, or RNAi. Often

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.490
revolutionary discoveries come from
scientists following an unusual

5
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:25.510
observation during their studies. For
doctors Andrew Fire and Craig Mello,

6
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.580
that came when they saw a mutant strain
of C. elegans, a type of worm commonly

7
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:35.850
used as an animal model, display an
unusual twitching behavior. They had

8
00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.700
been studying how gene expression is
controlled, but to understand their

9
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:44.400
surprising observation, we first have
to understand this critical process by

10
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.790
which DNA a is turned into proteins.
First DNA, which is made up of two

11
00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:54.930
complementary strands, is unzipped and
a complex of proteins turns one of

12
00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:59.920
these strands into a single strand of
RNA known as messenger RNA, or mRNA.

13
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.010
This mRNA is then read by a different
complex of proteins, resulting in the

14
00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:11.210
assembly of a functional protein. So
doctors Fire and Mello decided to see

15
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.660
if introducing various forms of RNA
sequences that matched an RNA

16
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:22.250
responsible for a worm muscle protein
would block production of that protein

17
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:27.490
and result in the twitching behavior.
First, they introduced single-stranded

18
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.500
RNA, or ssRNA,

19
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.210
that was complementary to the target
RNA, thinking that this would block the

20
00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:35.700
cell's ability to read the RNA.

21
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.190
But to their surprise, the worms were
completely normal. Then they tried

22
00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:46.720
pairing the ssRNA with a partner strand
to create double-stranded RNA and

23
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.660
injected this double-stranded RNA into
the worms, as opposed to

24
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:56.010
single-stranded RNA. To their surprise,
the double stranded RNA caused the

25
00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.960
worms to twitch, signaling that the
protein had been turned off or silenced.

26
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:06.360
This unexpected observation set off a
chain of experiments which ultimately

27
00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:11.620
revealed that double-stranded RNA is a
signal for cells to initiate a series

28
00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:16.360
of molecular events that leads to the
destruction of a matched RNA sequence.

29
00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:22.290
Doing so prevents production of the
protein encoded by the RNA. This entire

30
00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:28.280
process is known as RNA interference,
or RNAi. Doctors Fire and Mello

31
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.720
published their initial findings in
1998 and won the Nobel Prize in

32
00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:38.250
Physiology or Medicine in 2006. But why
was this discovery so important?

33
00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.530
In the years that followed Dr. Fire and
Dr. Mello's initial findings,

34
00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.810
scientists began to understand that
RNAi is a process that naturally

35
00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:54.020
evolved in many organisms for a variety
of functions. For example, cells use it

36
00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:59.000
as a defense mechanism against viruses.
When viruses cause infection, they

37
00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:04.140
inject their genetic material into
cells. Many viruses have genomes made

38
00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.190
up of double-stranded RNA, which
activates the cell's RNAi machinery to

39
00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:14.290
quickly degrade the viral RNA and
prevent the spread of infection. RNAi

40
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:19.740
is also an elegant form of genetic
regulation. MicroRNAs are small RNA

41
00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:25.000
fragments encoded throughout the genome
of many organisms, and they can bind to

42
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.960
certain RNA molecules creating
double-stranded RNA, which ultimately

43
00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:35.290
blocks protein production. MicroRNAs
play critical roles in numerous

44
00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.950
cellular functions, such as cell growth
and differentiation, and have been

45
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.260
implicated in various diseases such as
cancer.

46
00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:50.080
Today RNAi is also used as a strategy
to remove the function of a specific

47
00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:55.640
gene and examine how that impacts the
cell. For example, scientists can use

48
00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:01.950
small interfering RNAs, or siRNAs, a
type of RNAi technology, to pair with

49
00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.660
and degrade a natural RNA in the cell.

50
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.200
Doing so allows researchers to identify
the role of a specific protein by

51
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.760
studying the cellular outcomes in its
absence.

52
00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:19.390
RNAi also has found its way into
medicine. In 2018, patisiran, an siRNA

53
00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:24.990
molecule, was approved for a disease of
the nervous system caused by hereditary

54
00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.730
transthyretin-mediated amyloidosis
(hATTR).

55
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:33.020
This disease is characterized by the
abnormal build up of a protein called

56
00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:37.600
transthyretin in the nervous system,
which can impact movement, heart rate

57
00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.550
and digestion.

58
00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:44.160
Patisiran directly targets the RNA
responsible for making transthyretin,

59
00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.980
thereby limiting accumulation of the
disease-causing protein. Patients who

60
00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:53.400
received patisiran in a clinical trial
had significantly improved muscle

61
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.760
strength and movement, compared to
people receiving a placebo.

62
00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:03.140
This landmark approval ushered in an
entirely new class of medicines for

63
00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:08.190
patients who had few options beforehand.
Looking back, it's pretty incredible

64
00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.560
that an entirely new class of medicines
all started with some twitching worms.

65
00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:16.740
But this is actually very typical of
how science works. Breakthrough

66
00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:20.400
discoveries begin with curious
scientists following unusual

67
00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:25.930
observations. Albert Einstein summed it
up best by saying, "I have no special

68
00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:29.160
talents, I am only passionately
curious."

